 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 18,
   "text": "DERWENTWATER. 'Queen of the Lakes', three miles long, with islands, a floating island of weed in late summer, and boats round it every hour. Not the largest lake in the district. The prettiest, we're told."
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "THE MOOT HALL. Built in 1813, with a clock tower and a very pleasant way of standing in the middle of the road. 'Moot' is an old word for a meeting. It's now the tourist information centre."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "CUMBERLAND PENCIL MUSEUM. Pencils have been made in Keswick since 1832, from graphite found in the hills. Inside is one of the world's longest coloured pencils, nearly eight metres. Exit through the gift shop."
  },
  {
   "x": 11,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "NOW SHARPENING. Please queue. It's a long queue and all points. (We are sorry. We have been told to leave it in.)"
  },
  {
   "x": 51,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "CASTLERIGG STONE CIRCLE. About forty stones in a ring on a hilltop, over five thousand years old, with fells in every direction. Nobody knows quite what it was for. A good guess is a very early meeting. Some things never change."
  },
  {
   "x": 6,
   "y": 25,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Graphite found in Borrowdale in the 1500s made Keswick a pencil town for centuries.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": 0.475,
   "plaque": "keswick_borrowdale_graphite",
   "title": "Borrowdale Graphite"
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 27,
   "plaque": "keswick_name",
   "title": "Name: Keswick",
   "text": "Keswick comes from Old English 'cese' and 'wic': the cheese farm. Further south the same words became Chiswick, as in the bit of London, but up here the Norse-flavoured speech kept the hard K. So the town is, at heart, a dairy."
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 41,
   "plaque": "keswick_southey",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Southey and the Bears",
   "text": "The poet Robert Southey lived at Greta Hall in Keswick for forty years and was Poet Laureate from 1813. He also published the first known printed version of The Story of the Three Bears, in 1837. In his telling there is no Goldilocks, just a rude old woman who helps herself to the porridge."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 38,
   "plaque": "keswick_red_squirrels",
   "title": "Red Squirrels",
   "text": "The woods round Keswick are one of England's last strongholds of the red squirrel. Greys, brought over from America in the 1800s, carry a pox that kills reds, so local volunteers keep a sharp eye out. If you spot a small russet one with ear tufts, count yourself lucky."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 21,
   "plaque": "keswick_skiddaw",
   "title": "Skiddaw",
   "text": "Skiddaw, 931 metres, looms over Keswick. Its rocks, the Skiddaw Slates, are mudstones laid down on an ancient sea floor nearly 500 million years ago, among the oldest in the Lake District. It is a gentle giant: a long, steady path takes you all the way to the top."
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 19,
